<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llŷn Peninsula</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lleyn Peninsula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Llŷn Peninsula extends about 50 kilometres into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> from north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, in the county of Gwynedd. It has many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillforts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trer-ceiri">Tre'r Ceiri</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a pilgrim route to Bardsey Island. Ptolemy's headland of the Gangani is generally placed at its tip, and Welsh remains the everyday language of many of its people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/llyn-peninsula.jpg" alt="Llŷn Peninsula" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chembeth · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ll%C5%B7n_Peninsula_near_Nefyn,_North_Wales,_UK.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
